# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# VELORA · .gitignore
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# This file was EMPTY. Everything below it is something that must never enter a
# commit, and the repository has at least one of each already on disk:
#
#   storage/logs/velora.log   contains client IPs and session-id prefixes
#   .env                      does not exist yet — that is the point
#
# An empty .gitignore on a repository that is about to be published is not a
# neutral default. It means the first person to run the site creates .env with
# the database password and the admin credential hash, and every subsequent
# `git add -A` offers to publish them.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ─── Secrets · the reason this file exists ──────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Anything that looks like a credential dump, regardless of extension
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa*
credentials.json
secrets.json

# ─── Runtime state · written by the application, never source ───────────────
# .gitkeep stays: it is what makes these directories exist in a fresh clone,
# and product_upload_dir() depends on storage/uploads/products existing.
#
# The .htaccess files must ALSO stay, and `*` matches dotfiles. They are the
# deny-execute control for the upload directory — the one layer that can stop a
# file already on disk from being executed, which no amount of PHP validation
# can do. Without them in the repository the security fix exists only on this
# machine and does not deploy.
storage/logs/*
!storage/logs/.gitkeep
storage/cache/*
!storage/cache/.gitkeep
storage/uploads/products/*
!storage/uploads/products/.gitkeep
!storage/uploads/products/.htaccess
!storage/uploads/.htaccess
!storage/.htaccess

# Lock files: a stale lock in a clone is a checkout that blocks forever.
*.lock
products.json.lock

# ─── Audit leftovers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Created by this session's audit. Blocked by the .htaccess FilesMatch rule,
# which is defence in depth — the real reason to ignore them is that they are
# a 500 KB copy of the application sitting in the tree.
*.bak
*.orig
*.tmp
*.rej
*~
.*.swp

# ─── Local tooling ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.idea/
.vscode/
*.sublime-workspace
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store

# ─── Node ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
# vendor/ is NOT ignored on purpose: js/vendor/three.module.js is a committed,
# pinned build the storefront loads directly, and an offline install of a shop
# that must render on first paint cannot depend on a package manager.

# ─── NOT ignored, deliberately ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# .env.example      — the documented template, values blank by design
# products.json     — the catalogue, the actual product data of this shop
# db_schema.sql     — the schema, needed to install
# js/vendor/        — pinned third-party build, shipped for offline first paint
# .htaccess         — it carries the security policy
#
# If the catalogue ever holds data you would rather not publish, remove the
# products.json line above rather than assuming it is safe by default.